#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121031 :tutorial/content "There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nour equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nLU\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we set $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that $[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Finally, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ We have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a20df11-b376-5315-8a2a-b65f70b54723" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3181 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
